  "verdict": "The gap is the radome's PEEK column (11.85 mm to the pocket floor) minus the board stack hanging from it, so it moves by 0.28–0.33 µm/K with both together and 0.56 µm/K when the radome leads: over the capsule requirement it closes by up to 20 µm in the cold and opens by 22 µm in the heat (33 / 36 µm with the gradient). Against 52's minimal mitigation (L1 spacer only, ±0.112 mm, which leaves 28 µm to each window edge) the isothermal swing holds the 50–70 % window (margins 6 / 8 µm) and the gradient bound breaks it (-8 / -5 µm) — L1 tolerates a radome leading the stack by about 23 K at the extremes; the bayonet hard-stop mitigation (±0.071 mm) holds it even then. Daily, 71's capsule (no night-sky cooling, so a lower bound) swings by a median 14.5 K and a 95th percentile 24.9 K, a pin stroke of 4.8–13.8 µm — 0.7–2.0 % of the minimum preload once a day — and a pin at radius r slides by 0.95–1.17 µm per mm of r on a 95th-percentile day. The thermal swing is added linearly to 52's RSS residual (a systematic term on a statistical one — the conservative stacking), and the two axial terms not computed both SHRINK it — the pocket gasket (placeholder) expands as the PEEK does and pushes the stack the other way, the pin body (no primary; 0.0006 µm/K per ppm/K) pushes its tip against the gap's change — so leaving them out keeps this a bound. Not computed either: the stack's real centring and the centre pad's wire path (HW.34)."
